WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.451
Euclid's Elements, Book One, Proposition Seventeen.

2
00:00:03.451 --> 00:00:07.789
In any triangle, the sum of any two angles is less than two right angles.

3
00:00:07.789 --> 00:00:13.970
Given triangle A B C, we prove that any two of its angles taken together are less than two right angles.

4
00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:17.760
Extend side B C continuously to point D.

5
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:26.442
Since angle A C D is an exterior angle of triangle A B C, it is greater than the interior and opposite angle A B C.

6
00:00:26.442 --> 00:00:34.273
Add angle A C B to each, so angles A C D and A C B together are greater than angles A B C and B C A together.

7
00:00:34.273 --> 00:00:41.313
Yet since segment A C stands on line B D, adjacent angles A C D and A C B sum to two right angles.

8
00:00:41.313 --> 00:00:50.940
Therefore angles A B C and B C A together are less than two right angles, and similarly for the other pairs, as was required to prove.
